# The PHI path pattern, in one place.
#
# Read by .githooks/pre-commit (before a commit) and .github/workflows/ci.yml
# (tree and history, on every push). It used to be written out in each of them
# separately, and they drifted: the corpus moved from data/ to free_texts/ and
# the hook was updated while CI was not, so for a period `free_texts/synthetic/
# *.txt` (named `notes_txt/` at the time) -- the raw clinical notes -- passed
# the check CI's own comment calls "what makes the PHI gate non-optional".
# tests/test_phi_guard.py now fails if
# any caller stops reading this file, or if this pattern stops matching the
# corpus layout.
#
# Format: comment and blank lines are ignored; the first line that is neither
# is the pattern, as a POSIX extended regex matched case-insensitively against
# repository-relative paths. Keep it to one line -- every consumer reads it
# with `grep -E`, and a multi-line pattern would silently use only the first.
#
# What each part covers, and why it fails closed:
#
#   ^(data|out|free_texts)/
#       Every directory that has ever held the corpus or anything derived from
#       it. `data/` holds only built dictionaries now and stays listed anyway:
#       an ignore rule that has to be re-added later is one that will be
#       missing when it matters.
#
#   \.(sqlite|jsonl|xmi|csv|tsv|txt|db|parquet)$
#       Suffix cover for the same artifacts written somewhere new. A new output
#       directory should fail closed rather than be discovered. `csv` is here
#       because adjudication review files quote whole sentences of note text
#       and a reviewer working in Excel saves them wherever is convenient;
#       `txt` because the raw notes are .txt; `parquet` because a Parquet
#       export holds the same table the CSV export does,
#       verbatim `text` column included, and a binary file gives a reviewer
#       even less chance of noticing note text in it by eye.
#
#   (^|/)(design|_verdicts|_pairs|run_manifest)\.json$
#       The adjudication sidecars, by name rather than by suffix. A blanket
#       \.json$ would also block ordinary project files (.vscode/settings.json,
#       a devcontainer), so the filenames that carry corpus offsets and
#       verdicts are named instead. This is the one part that is narrow on
#       purpose; widen it if a further sidecar appears.
#
#       `run_manifest` is that further sidecar: `python -m umlsmatch --out-dir`
#       writes one per run, listing every source path it processed, and in this
#       corpus those filenames are patient and note identifiers. The annotation
#       files beside it are already covered by the .jsonl and .txt suffixes --
#       the manifest was the only part of a run directory that would have
#       passed. It is named `run_manifest` rather than `manifest` precisely so
#       it can be listed here without catching unrelated files.
#
# Adding a pattern here needs no change to the hook or to CI. Adding one
# anywhere else does, which is the point.
#
# Exceptions live in .githooks/phi-allow.pattern, not here: a path is refused
# when it matches this file AND does not match that one. Keep it that way. The
# two are not symmetric -- this file is safe to widen and dangerous to narrow,
# that one is the reverse -- and folding them together would lose the property
# that everything in this file is a refusal.
^(data|out|free_texts)/|\.(sqlite|jsonl|xmi|csv|tsv|txt|db|parquet)$|(^|/)(design|_verdicts|_pairs|run_manifest)\.json$
